      "status": "coded",
      "rationale": "The CSRM is a reusable CubeSat reference/logical architecture intended as a starting point for mission teams, not a single mission-specific model. It is mission-agnostic because teams populate mission-specific stakeholders, requirements, behaviors, and measures. It uses high-level logical abstractions and does not impose physical implementation constraints. The paper also supports a non-detailed/descriptive behavior interpretation because the model is not pre-populated with behaviors and the authors state that dynamic/performance analysis requires external mission models and further research. Do not add interface-focused model or specific-mission model with reusable method only.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "The International Council on Systems Engineering (INCOSE) Space System Working Group (SSWG) has created the CubeSat System Reference Model (CSRM), a representation of the logical architecture of a CubeSat system, intended to be used by system architects and engineers as a starting point as they develop the physical architecture of the Space and Ground segments of the CubeSat mission of interest to them.",
          "location": "Abstract"
        },
        {
          "quote": "The logical components are abstractions of the physical components that perform the system functionality without imposing implementation constraints.",
          "location": "Section 2"
        },
        {
          "quote": "The CSRM is a repository for systems engineering artifacts. However, it is not pre-populated with specific stakeholders, technical measures, behaviors, and requirements. That is the job of the CubeSat mission development team, specific to their needs and objectives.",
          "location": "Section 2"
        },
        {
          "quote": "A mission-specific CubeSat team downloads the CSRM specification and files from OMG for import it into their own graphical modeling tool.",
          "location": "Section 2"
        },
        {
          "quote": "While these products enable a complete description of a mission from multiple perspectives, they do not enable detailed analysis of the performance of that mission...Proper analysis requires definition and analysis of external mission models.",
          "location": "Section 4"
        }
      ],
      "confidence": "high"
    },
